            <ArticleTitle>Role of aeroallergen nasal challenge in asthmatic children.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>17-22</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.aller.2010.03.003</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The majority of allergic asthmatic patients are sensitised to several aeroallergens. Discrimination of the clinically relevant allergen is essential for the correct use of immunotherapy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To investigate nasal challenge and its role in screening clinically relevant allergens in asthmatic children.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Aeroallergen nasal challenge was performed in five different groups of patients (asthma; asthma &amp; rhinitis; rhinitis; atopic controls; and non-atopic controls). Differences between groups after challenge were evaluated by means of spirometry and acoustic rhinometry.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Nasal challenge was performed in 125 patients, 25 per group. The positive nasal response of immediate type was recorded in 21 patients with asthma only (P&lt;0.001), 18 with asthma and rhinitis (P&lt;0.001), 19 with rhinitis (P&lt;0.001), two atopic control patients and in no healthy control patients. However, no differences were observed between the asthma group and the groups with rhinitis symptoms. The risk of a positive challenge was much higher in the asthma without rhinitis group compared to atopic controls (OR 29.57; 95%CI: 5.47-159.97).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Aeroallergen nasal challenge is a safe technique in asthmatic children and could be useful in establishing the clinically relevant allergen even in the absence of rhinitis.</AbstractText>
